{"split": "train", "row_index": 754, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 755, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "RID of the world’s injustice, and his pain,\nHe rests at last beneath God’s veil of blue:\nTaken from life when life and love were new\nThe youngest of the martyrs here is lain,\nFair as Sebastian, and as early slain.\nNo cypress shades his grave, no funeral yew,\nBut gentle violets weeping with the dew\nWeave on his bones an ever-blossoming chain.\nO proudest heart that broke for misery!\nO sweetest lips since those of Mitylene!\nO poet-painter of our English Land!\nThy name was writ in water—it shall stand:\nAnd tears like mine will keep thy memory green,\nAs Isabella did her Basil-tree.", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 756, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 757, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 758, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 759, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "TREAD lightly, she is near\nUnder the snow,\nSpeak gently, she can hear\nThe daisies grow.\n\nAll her bright golden hair\nTarnished with rust,\nShe that was young and fair\nFallen to dust.\n\nLily-like, white as snow,\nShe hardly knew\nShe was a woman, so\nSweetly she grew.\n\nCoffin-board, heavy stone,\nLie on her breast,\nI vex my heart alone,\nShe is at rest.\n\nPeace, Peace, she cannot hear\nLyre or sonnet,\nAll my life’s buried here,\nHeap earth upon it.", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 760, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "delete", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "source/front-matter contamination"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 761, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "LIKE burnt-out torches by a sick man’s bed\nGaunt cypress-trees stand round the sun-bleached stone;\nHere doth the little night-owl make her throne,\nAnd the slight lizard show his jewelled head.\nAnd, where the chaliced poppies flame to red,\nIn the still chamber of yon pyramid\nSurely some Old-World Sphinx lurks darkly hid,\nGrim warder of this pleasaunce of the dead.\n\nAh! sweet indeed to rest within the womb\nOf Earth, great mother of eternal sleep,\nBut sweeter far for thee a restless tomb\nIn the blue cavern of an echoing deep,\nOr where the tall ships founder in the gloom\nAgainst the rocks of some wave-shattered steep.", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 762, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
