{"split": "train", "row_index": 772, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 773, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "THE Gods are dead: no longer do we bring\nTo grey-eyed Pallas crowns of olive-leaves!\nDemeter’s child no more hath tithe of sheaves,\nAnd in the noon the careless shepherds sing,\nFor Pan is dead, and all the wantoning\nBy secret glade and devious haunt is o’er:\nYoung Hylas seeks the water-springs no more;\nGreat Pan is dead, and Mary’s son is King.\n\nAnd yet—perchance in this sea-trancèd isle,\nChewing the bitter fruit of memory,\nSome God lies hidden in the asphodel.\nAh Love! if such there be, then it were well\nFor us to fly his anger: nay, but see,\nThe leaves are stirring: let us watch awhile.", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 774, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 775, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "ROME! what a scroll of History thine has been;\nIn the first days thy sword republican\nRuled the whole world for many an age’s span:\nThen of the peoples wert thou royal Queen,\nTill in thy streets the bearded Goth was seen;\nAnd now upon thy walls the breezes fan\n(Ah, city crowned by God, discrowned by man!)\nThe hated flag of red and white and green.\nWhen was thy glory! when in search for power\nThine eagles flew to greet the double sun,\nAnd the wild nations shuddered at thy rod?\nNay, but thy glory tarried for this hour,\nWhen pilgrims kneel before the Holy One,\nThe prisoned shepherd of the Church of God.", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 776, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "delete", "reason": "duplicate", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "exact duplicate of row 751"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 777, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 778, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "keep", "reason": "clean", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": null, "notes": "clean poem"}
{"split": "train", "row_index": 779, "author": "oscar_wilde", "decision": "edit", "reason": "source_artifact", "confidence": "high", "ref_text_clean": null, "target_text_clean": "THE sea was sapphire coloured, and the sky\nBurned like a heated opal through the air;\nWe hoisted sail; the wind was blowing fair\nFor the blue lands that to the eastward lie.\nFrom the steep prow I marked with quickening eye\nZakynthos, every olive grove and creek,\nIthaca’s cliff, Lycaon’s snowy peak,\nAnd all the flower-strewn hills of Arcady.\nThe flapping of the sail against the mast,\nThe ripple of the water on the side,\nThe ripple of girls’ laughter at the stern,\nThe only sounds:—when ’gan the West to burn,\nAnd a red sun upon the seas to ride,\nI stood upon the soil of Greece at last!", "notes": "removed trailing location line"}
